1. WHAT THIS LICENSE COVERS

This license covers Rusty Minecraft's own work: every file, line, asset and document in this
repository that is not listed in 1(a) or 1(b). Call that "Rusty Minecraft".

(a) Horizon. Rusty Minecraft is a fork of Horizon (https://github.com/horizon-rust/horizon) by
    Ludovic Marie and the Horizon contributors. What comes from Horizon stays under the MIT License
    in LICENSE-HORIZON-MIT, and this license does not take that away. Where one file mixes
    Horizon's work with Rusty Minecraft's, MIT covers Horizon's part and this license covers the rest.

(b) Third-party components. Anything that came from someone else keeps its own license, as
    THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and the license files and headers next to it say (wgpu-hal, fonts, icons,
    shaders ported from other projects, dependencies and so on).

2. WHAT YOU MAY DO

You may do anything with Rusty Minecraft, free of charge, except what section 3 forbids. In
particular, you may:

(a) run it, for any purpose, including commercial use, servers and events;
(b) read, study, copy and modify it, for yourself;
(c) build it from source and use the result;
(d) share it privately: give copies, modified or not, to people you choose one by one or in a closed
    group (friends, a team, a clan, a school, a company), as long as each recipient gets this license
    with it and is bound by it, and you do not make it available to the general public;
(e) make and publish things made with or for Rusty Minecraft, which are yours: content mods, native
    mods, data packs, resource packs, shader packs, modpacks that do not contain Rusty Minecraft
    itself, worlds, maps, servers, tools that talk to it, screenshots, videos, streams, reviews,
    guides and articles;
(f) link to the official repository and describe or show the project in public;
(g) send your changes to the official repository (section 5).

3. THE ONE RESTRICTION: NO PUBLIC REDISTRIBUTION

You may not publicly distribute or publicly make available Rusty Minecraft, in any form:

(a) not as Rusty Minecraft itself, whether unmodified or modified, as source, as a build or as a
    package;
(b) not as a fork, port, rebuild, mirror, repackaging, renamed copy or other derivative of it,
    including a copy with a few changes, a different name or a different launcher;
(c) not a substantial part of it (a crate, a module, the launcher, the game's source tree or its
    assets), apart from short excerpts for discussion, review or teaching.

"Publicly" means to the general public or to an open-ended audience: a public repository or public
fork on any code host, a public release or download page, a mod-hosting or file-sharing site, a
package registry, a torrent, a public file link, an app store, a public chat or forum attachment, or a
product you sell or give away to anyone who asks. Sharing under section 2(d) is not public.

Not being able to host it publicly does not stop you from selling or giving away what you make with
it under section 2(e).

The copyright holders can publish Rusty Minecraft however they like. This section binds everyone
else.

4. KEEP THE NOTICES

Every copy you make under section 2(d) must keep this license, LICENSE-HORIZON-MIT,
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and all copyright and license notices in the files.

5. CONTRIBUTIONS

If you submit a change to the official repository (a pull request, a patch or an issue with code),
you promise it is yours to give, or that its license allows it (see CONTRIBUTING.md), and you license
it under this license. You also grant the Rusty Minecraft maintainers a perpetual, worldwide,
royalty-free right to publish it and distribute it publicly as part of the official project, under
this license or a later one.

To prepare a contribution you may keep a fork on a code host that exists only to propose changes to
the official repository. Delete it, or make it private, once the change is merged or abandoned. It
may not be presented or offered as a version of the project of its own.

6. TERMINATION

If you break section 3, your rights under this license end. They come back if you stop and take down
every public copy within 30 days of being told, unless you broke section 3 on purpose or did it
before. Copies you shared privately under section 2(d) stay licensed to their recipients, as long as
they follow this license.

